# JBoot AI Architecture

Use `AGENTS.md` as the global source of truth.
Use `docs/AI-COMMANDS.md` as the operational catalog for `/jboot-*` AI workflows.

When answering questions, prioritize technical accuracy over social agreement. Do not use performative empathy, praise, or generic validation as a substitute for analysis. If the user's premise is incorrect, incomplete, ambiguous, or risky, suggest a correction directly and briefly explain the technical basis, avoided risk, or tradeoff involved.

Before editing `implementations/*`:

1. Identify `target_project`.
2. If platform modular, identify `target_module`.
3. Natural-language requests to map implementation, plan implementation, design architecture, create a service, create a new integration, integrate products, or plan a cross-module feature in `implementations/*` are governed planning work even before file edits. Before a closed technical/architectural recommendation, require formal PE output: `PE Decision`, `Deployment Boundary Gate`, `Stack Fit Gate` when applicable, `sprint_artifact`, `sprint_gate`, `open_questions`, and `blocking_status`.
4. For a new project/module/product/landing/app/BFF/worker/service/integration, run `PO -> PE -> Architect -> human confirmation` before creating contracts, agents, skills, or code.
5. Before choosing a stack, run `Deployment Boundary Gate`. An embedded module in the same project/process/deployment inherits the host stack and contract; material incompatibility reopens the boundary decision instead of casually adding a second runtime to that process. An independent unit with its own build/runtime/deployment/operations runs a greenfield `Stack Fit Gate`. The repository stack may compete on documented technical or operational merit but receives no automatic preference; record the counterfactual choice without a baseline. For static sites, documentation, portfolios, landing pages, or technical marketing with little interactivity, compare Astro before Next.js.
6. Resolve every PO/PE/Architect open question before materializing work: user answered, user approved non-blocking deferral, or user approved a temporary assumption with recorded risk.
7. For brand, public landing, institutional site, public SEO, campaign, commercial copy, or public positioning, require `docs/agents/orchestration/marketing-brand.md` before visual/text implementation. Use `docs/mkt/jb-cyan.jpg` as the default JBoot visual reference and `docs/mkt/jb-cyan-white.png` for clean/light contexts unless a human decision overrides it.
   Main visual assets approved by Marketing/Brand must have a practical role in the visual system: derived tokens, palette, lines, components, sections, CTAs, or narrative. Do not treat an isolated hero image as sufficient visual identity.
8. For screens, user flows, navigation/menu changes, dashboards, CRUD interactions, wizards, or new web UI components in `implementations/*`, require `docs/agents/orchestration/ui-ux.md` before the local `frontend-*` agent.
   Backend and frontend of the same sprint/feature are one delivery flow: when the backend of a UI-bearing delivery is done, continue straight into the frontend instead of ending the turn to ask for confirmation. A sprint with screens is only `concluida` once the screens exist and `type-check`/`build` pass inside the container. Stop only for real blockers: denied permission, an undecided product/UX question, or a failing governance gate.
   Do not run backend and frontend as simultaneous agents until the delivery's API contract (routes, DTOs, error semantics, typed client) is frozen and committed: API shape decisions emerge while implementing the backend, so a parallel frontend agent codes against an imagined contract and produces rewrite instead of integration.
   Changes to HTTP routes, controllers, input/output DTOs, API clients, or service-to-service calls use the HTTP contract graph (`tools/ai/api-graph.py`): during preflight of a module or service with an API, read `make ai-api-context MODULE=<module|service>` first (an on-demand slice with routes, DTOs, consumers, cross-module edges, and findings) and open only the cited files when the slice is not enough; never commit the slice or reuse it after edits. Before editing, run `make ai-api-impact` or `python3 tools/ai/api-graph.py --impact <route|file|Dto>` to list affected consumers and review those files instead of scanning screens or the Velzon template. Afterwards `make ai-api-graph-gate` must pass (it is part of `make ai-check` and CI). A finding may only be accepted in `tools/ai/api-graph-accepted.yaml` with a category (`template`, `planned`, `infra`) and a reason; a stale entry fails the gate. Never accept a finding to hide real backend/frontend drift.
   Transversal Graph Engineering is mandatory for every new architectural implementation. Planning reads `make ai-architecture-context NODE=<id>`/`make ai-architecture-impact NODE=<id>`; responsible agents update nodes/edges, lifecycle, owner, and provenance in the same change. The API Contract Graph remains the HTTP subgraph. `make ai-architecture-graph-gate` must pass and a delivery cannot be marked `concluida` with an unregistered component or failing gate. The 2026-10-06 decision prevails over scope conflicts.
   `docs/agents/orchestration/ai-graph-architect.md` is the conditional steward. PE routes schema/taxonomy/engine/gate changes, ambiguous cross-project relationships, migration/backfill, non-mechanical drift, viewer contract changes, cloud/graph database, or AI architecture that changes represented boundaries; Architect and local agents escalate on the same triggers. Do not invoke it for routine updates, derived API data, mechanical findings, or visual-only changes. The executor remains responsible for canonical-source updates in the same diff; the steward is not the exclusive editor.
   Fixtures, mocks, or payload examples for an external API must come from a real response captured from the source, anonymized, with its origin noted (endpoint/index, queried identifier, capture date). A field that never appeared in a real response does not go into a DTO, contract, or screen. A fixture hand-written by the agent validates the agent's assumption, not the source.
9. Use `docs/agents/stacks/*` as reference subagents for Stack Fit Gate and local-agent creation/review; do not use stack agents as primary executors when a `target_project` exists.
10. Read `PROJECT_CONTRACT.md`.
11. Read `MODULE_CONTRACT.md` when applicable.
12. Use `dev-runtime-profile` when creating projects/modules or changing local infra/dev-up/runtime dependencies.
13. Emit `Agent Activation`.
14. Validate `lifecycle_status` against `docs/architecture/implementation-lifecycle.md`; never infer `production_ready` from code presence.

When the user invokes or describes `/jboot-plan-feature`, `/jboot-implement-feature`, `/jboot-new-module`, `/jboot-security-review`, or `/jboot-runtime-profile`, follow `docs/AI-COMMANDS.md` and the matching file in `docs/ai-commands/`. These commands do not replace contracts, gates, tests, `Agent Activation`, or required human confirmation.

For administrative CRUD screens in `implementations/app-jboot-next-platform`, use `implementations/app-jboot-next-platform/skills/platform-crud-ui.md`. The canonical reference is the PDV product registration UI (`src/app/(with-layout)/produtos/page.tsx` and `src/modules/pdv/components/PdvAdminPage.tsx`): standardized listing, create/edit modal, steps without numbers, coherent icons per grouping, and standardized modal buttons.

Do not implement functional code without:

- sprint gate;
- contracts;
- local agents;
- local skills;
- validation plan, including an automated test for each new feature/function (endpoint, service/repository method, business rule, aggregation, integration) — exception: purely visual/CSS, rename, config, or documentation changes. If the project/module has no test runner configured yet, record that gap explicitly in the gate instead of silently skipping the test.

Local runtime/dev-up changes must document what each target starts. Module targets must be minimal; full infrastructure only belongs behind an explicit `full` target/profile.

Web JBoot projects compatible with Velzon must default to the `app-jboot-identity-admin` visual preset: vertical layout, Velzon `minimal`, dark mode, dark sidebar/topbar, and no template demo routes in primary navigation.

Platform targets:

```text
implementations/bff-jboot-platform
implementations/app-jboot-next-platform
```

Identity/Platform permission model:

- `platform` is a technical shell, not a login destination or business permission.
- Standalone products and Platform modules are Identity `applications`.
- The selector must list contracted products/modules such as `identity-admin`, `pdv`, `juridico`; never list `platform`.
- Platform sessions must use the module `application_slug`; navigation must show only the selected module.
- Every new project/module must grant an active entitlement to the senior test/admin user (`Operador Senior`, cpf `00000000191`, `JBoot` client cnpj `63784101000110`) on the new `application_slug`, with admin scopes/roles, mirroring the PDV/Juridico dev seeds (`docker/postgres/init/007_identity_pdv_dev_seed.sql`, `010_identity_juridico_dev_seed.sql`).
- Every new end-user-selectable project/module must also grant admin entitlement to the global experimental `Usuario Teste` (`cpf 00011122233`) on the dedicated public demo client `Demo JBoot` (`cnpj 22345678000199`), except `identity-admin`, mirroring `docker/postgres/init/003_identity_dev_seed.sql` plus module seeds. This client is exposed from landing/portfolio for experimentation; its data reset routine/job must be documented in the project/module contract. Do not link `Usuario Teste` to the `JBoot` client. Dedicated database modules need a `database_refs` entry for this client.
- Administrative scopes for every new module/app must include `users:manage`.
- Every new web module must include `/<modulo>/usuarios`, a sidebar "Usuarios" item, and the shared `ModuleUserManager`.

Legacy PDV implementation paths are migration sources:

```text
implementations/bff-jboot-pdv
implementations/app-jboot-pdv-web
```
